title: Setting ignore patterns
tags: [[File tracking]]
kind: guide
summary: Apply presets with mf ignore or the ignore:* commands, ignore a path or an extension from the file manager — and mind the inherited set.

!! From the command line

```
mf -n code ignore                          # the presets (ignore list)
mf -n code ignore list -d /src             # … and the patterns active on /src
mf -n code ignore add rust-build,node -d /src
mf -n code ignore remove node -d /src
mf -n code ignore set default              # replace the root's whole set
mf -n code ignore set -d /src              # no preset: clear /src's set
```

`-d` names the target directory, repository-relative (default: the root). `add`
appends the presets' patterns (skipping rows already there), `remove` deletes exactly
those, `set` replaces the whole set. Presets are comma- or space-separated.

!! In the GUI

Four commands mirror the verbs, each completing over the installed presets:
`ignore:list` (the presets and the target's active set, in the message panel — look
before you write), `ignore:add`, `ignore:remove`, `ignore:set`. The ''target'' is the
directory you are looking at: the file manager's current directory, else the selected
metarecord if it is a directory, else the root. The status message always names it
(`Ignore: +13 patterns on /music`).

The [[file manager|file-manager panel]] ''shows'' ignoring: an entry left untracked on purpose has a muted name
behind a small `⊘`, and its tooltip gives the cause — `ignored by <pattern>` with the
directory the set comes from, or `mf_watch = false` on the metarecord that decided (only
where that differs from the listed directory, else a fresh repository would mark every
row). It also ''offers'' it: a row's [[context menu|Context menus]] has an ''Ignore'' section — this path
exactly, every file with its extension here, or apply a preset. The patterns are built
from the path relative to the tracking scope, regex-escaped, and written onto the
''current directory'', not the row: an ignore set governs a subtree. The listing is
re-marked right after the write.

!! The inherited set

Sets are replaced, never merged: the nearest ancestor with at least one row wins. So
writing one pattern on a directory that has none drops the whole inherited set for its
subtree. Before such a write the GUI asks:

```
/music/live has no ignore patterns of its own; it inherits 13 from /.
  [Copy the 13 inherited patterns here, then apply]   (default)
  [Start from an empty set here]
  [Cancel]
```

Copying first makes the gesture mean "one more pattern here". From the command line,
read the set with `mf ignore list -d <dir>` and write what you want with `set` or `add`.

Ignoring never deletes a metarecord: it only stops future tracking
([[When watch or ignore changes]]).
